{"subjectId":"python","id":"serialization","title":"Serialization: JSON, CSV & pickle","group":"Errors, Modules & Files","order":50,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Turning Python objects into bytes or text and back — JSON for APIs, CSV for tables, TOML/INI for config, pickle for Python-only caches, and <code>struct</code> for binary formats.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Module</th><th>Human-readable</th><th>Cross-language</th><th>Safe on untrusted input</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>JSON</td><td><code>json</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>CSV</td><td><code>csv</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>TOML</td><td><code>tomllib</code> (read-only)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>INI</td><td><code>configparser</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Mostly</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>pickle</td><td><code>pickle</code></td><td>No</td><td><strong>Python only</strong></td><td><strong>No — can run code</strong></td></tr><tr><td>SQLite</td><td><code>sqlite3</code></td><td>No (queryable)</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Packed binary</td><td><code>struct</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes (you define the layout)</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-braces\"></i>JSON basics &amp; type mapping</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import json\nuser = {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"skills\": [\"python\", \"sql\"], \"active\": True, \"manager\": None}\ntext = json.dumps(user)                  # object → str\nprint(text)                              # {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"skills\": [\"python\", \"sql\"], \"active\": true, \"manager\": null}\nprint(json.loads(text)[\"skills\"][0])     # python\n\nprint(json.loads(json.dumps({1: (1, 2)})))   # {'1': [1, 2]}   ← int keys become strings, tuples become lists</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Python</th><th>JSON</th><th>Back to Python</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>dict</code></td><td>object <code>{}</code></td><td><code>dict</code> (keys always <code>str</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>list</code>, <code>tuple</code></td><td>array <code>[]</code></td><td><code>list</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>str</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>str</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>int</code>, <code>float</code></td><td>number</td><td><code>int</code> / <code>float</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>True</code> / <code>False</code></td><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td><td><code>bool</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>None</code></td><td><code>null</code></td><td><code>None</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>set</code>, <code>datetime</code>, <code>Decimal</code>, objects</td><td><strong>TypeError</strong></td><td>— convert first</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>dumps</code> / <code>loads</code> work with strings; <code>dump(obj, f)</code> / <code>load(f)</code> with open files.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-adjustments\"></i>Formatting options</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import json\ndata = {\"name\": \"Zoë\", \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"id\": 7}\n\nprint(json.dumps(data))                                         # {\"name\": \"Zo\\u00eb\", \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"id\": 7}\nprint(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, sort_keys=True))     # {\"id\": 7, \"name\": \"Zoë\", \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"]}\nprint(json.dumps(data, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(\",\", \":\")))   # {\"name\":\"Zoë\",\"tags\":[\"a\",\"b\"],\"id\":7}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import json\nprint(json.dumps({\"id\": 7, \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"]}, indent=2))</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>{\n  \"id\": 7,\n  \"tags\": [\n    \"a\",\n    \"b\"\n  ]\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>indent=2</code></td><td>Pretty-print (config files, debugging)</td></tr><tr><td><code>sort_keys=True</code></td><td>Stable key order — diff-friendly output, cache keys</td></tr><tr><td><code>ensure_ascii=False</code></td><td>Keep non-ASCII characters instead of <code>\\uXXXX</code> escapes</td></tr><tr><td><code>separators=(',', ':')</code></td><td>Most compact output (network payloads)</td></tr><tr><td><code>default=fn</code></td><td>Called for objects JSON can't encode</td></tr><tr><td><code>allow_nan=False</code></td><td>Reject <code>NaN</code>/<code>Infinity</code>, which are not valid JSON</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-transform\"></i>Custom types: <code>default</code> and <code>object_hook</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import json\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, asdict, is_dataclass\nfrom datetime import date\n\n@dataclass\nclass Event:\n    title: str\n    day: date\n    tags: set\n\ndef encode(obj):\n    if isinstance(obj, date):\n        return obj.isoformat()\n    if isinstance(obj, set):\n        return sorted(obj)\n    if is_dataclass(obj):\n        return asdict(obj)\n    raise TypeError(f\"{type(obj).__name__} is not JSON serializable\")\n\ntext = json.dumps(Event(\"launch\", date(2026, 9, 29), {\"b\", \"a\"}), default=encode)\nprint(text)                                  # {\"title\": \"launch\", \"day\": \"2026-09-29\", \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"]}\n\ndef decode(d):                               # called for every JSON object, innermost first\n    if \"day\" in d:\n        d[\"day\"] = date.fromisoformat(d[\"day\"])\n    return d\n\nback = json.loads(text, object_hook=decode)\nprint(back[\"day\"].year, type(back[\"day\"]).__name__)   # 2026 date</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import json\ntry:\n    json.loads(\"{'single': 'quotes'}\")        # Python-style dict text is NOT JSON\nexcept json.JSONDecodeError as e:\n    print(e.msg, \"at position\", e.pos)        # Expecting property name enclosed in double quotes at position 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-table\"></i>CSV: reading &amp; writing</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import csv, io\n\nrows = [{\"name\": \"Asha\", \"city\": \"Pune, MH\"}, {\"name\": \"Ravi\", \"city\": 'Say \"hi\"'}]\nbuf = io.StringIO()\nwriter = csv.DictWriter(buf, fieldnames=[\"name\", \"city\"])\nwriter.writeheader()\nwriter.writerows(rows)\nprint(buf.getvalue().splitlines())   # ['name,city', 'Asha,\"Pune, MH\"', 'Ravi,\"Say \"\"hi\"\"\"']   ← commas and quotes are escaped for you\n\nreader = csv.reader(io.StringIO(buf.getvalue()))\nheader = next(reader)\nprint(header, list(reader))       # ['name', 'city'] [['Asha', 'Pune, MH'], ['Ravi', 'Say \"hi\"']]\n\nraw = \"name,score\\nasha,91\\nravi,84\\n\"\nscores = [int(r[\"score\"]) for r in csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(raw))]\nprint(sum(scores) / len(scores))  # 87.5</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import csv, io\ntsv = \"a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n\"\nprint(list(csv.reader(io.StringIO(tsv), delimiter=\"\\t\")))    # [['a', 'b'], ['1', '2']]\n\nbuf = io.StringIO()\ncsv.writer(buf, quoting=csv.QUOTE_NONNUMERIC, lineterminator=\"\\n\").writerow([\"x\", 1.5, 2])\nprint(buf.getvalue().strip())                                # \"x\",1.5,2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Open CSV files with <code>open(path, \"w\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\")</code>. Without <code>newline=\"\"</code> you get blank lines between rows on Windows. And never split lines on <code>,</code> by hand — quoted fields contain commas.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Every CSV value is read as a <strong>string</strong> — convert types yourself. For heavy tabular work use pandas (<code>pd.read_csv</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-package\"></i>pickle — Python objects as bytes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import pickle\n\ndata = {\"ids\": (1, 2, 3), \"tags\": {\"py\"}, \"nested\": [b\"raw\", None, 3.5]}\nblob = pickle.dumps(data)                    # tuples, sets, bytes: all preserved\nrestored = pickle.loads(blob)\nprint(type(blob).__name__, restored == data, type(restored[\"ids\"]).__name__)   # bytes True tuple</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Security</div><p><strong>Never unpickle data from an untrusted source.</strong> A pickle can instruct the loader to call any function (via <code>__reduce__</code>), i.e. run arbitrary code. Use JSON for anything that crosses a trust boundary.</p></div>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>How it stores classes</h3><p>By <em>import path</em> (<code>module.ClassName</code>), not code — the class must be importable when loading, and renaming it breaks old pickles.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i>Good uses</h3><p>Caching intermediate results, passing objects between your own processes (<code>multiprocessing</code> uses pickle).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-x\"></i>Bad uses</h3><p>Long-term storage, APIs, anything shared across Python versions or teams.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-archive\"></i><code>shelve</code></h3><p>A persistent dict backed by pickle: <code>with shelve.open('cache') as db: db['k'] = obj</code>.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>sqlite3 — a database in the standard library</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import sqlite3\n\ncon = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")            # or a file path\ncon.execute(\"CREATE TABLE users (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT)\")\nwith con:                                    # commits on success, rolls back on error\n    con.executemany(\"INSERT INTO users (name) VALUES (?)\", [(\"Asha\",), (\"Ravi\",)])\nprint(con.execute(\"SELECT id, name FROM users ORDER BY id\").fetchall())   # [(1, 'Asha'), (2, 'Ravi')]\nprint(con.execute(\"SELECT name FROM users WHERE id = ?\", (2,)).fetchone())  # ('Ravi',)\ncon.close()</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Always pass values as parameters (<code>?</code>), never with f-strings — string-built SQL is an injection hole.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-settings\"></i>Config files: TOML &amp; INI</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import tomllib, configparser\n\ncfg = tomllib.loads(\"\"\"\n[server]\nhost = \"0.0.0.0\"\nport = 8080\ndebug = false\n\n[database]\npool = { min = 1, max = 5 }\n\"\"\")\nprint(cfg[\"server\"][\"port\"], cfg[\"server\"][\"debug\"], cfg[\"database\"][\"pool\"][\"max\"])   # 8080 False 5\n\ncp = configparser.ConfigParser()\ncp.read_string(\"\"\"\n[app]\nname = notes\nworkers = 4\ndebug = yes\n\"\"\")\nprint(cp[\"app\"][\"name\"], cp.getint(\"app\", \"workers\"), cp.getboolean(\"app\", \"debug\"))   # notes 4 True</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>TOML has real types (ints, booleans, dates, arrays); INI values are all strings until you call <code>getint</code> / <code>getboolean</code>. <code>tomllib</code> (3.11+) only reads — write TOML with the third-party <code>tomli-w</code>. Use <code>tomllib.load</code> with a file opened in <strong>binary</strong> mode.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-binary\"></i>Binary layouts with struct</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import struct\n\n# \"&lt;\" little-endian, H = unsigned 16-bit, I = unsigned 32-bit, f = 32-bit float\npacked = struct.pack(\"&lt;HIf\", 7, 65536, 1.5)\nprint(len(packed), packed.hex())             # 10 0700000001000000c03f\nprint(struct.unpack(\"&lt;HIf\", packed))         # (7, 65536, 1.5)\nprint(struct.calcsize(\"&lt;HIf\"), struct.calcsize(\"HIf\"))   # 10 12   ← native mode adds alignment padding</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>JSON vs pickle — when would you use each?</summary><div><p>JSON for data exchanged with other programs or stored long-term: it's text, language-neutral and safe to parse. Pickle only for short-lived, trusted, Python-to-Python data (caches, IPC) — it handles almost any Python object but is insecure and version-fragile.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you serialize a datetime or a custom object to JSON?</summary><div><p>Pass <code>default=</code> to <code>json.dumps</code> — a function that converts unsupported objects (e.g. <code>dt.isoformat()</code>, <code>dataclasses.asdict(obj)</code>) — or subclass <code>json.JSONEncoder</code>. Decode with <code>object_hook=</code> to turn the dicts back into rich types.</p></div></details></section>"}